  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale  \n   We tackle the problem by evolving a population of candidate functions through a two-stage process: coarse clustering-based search and fine-grained local optimization. This hybrid approach leverages dimensionality reduction via clustering to navigate the complex function space efficiently, followed by gradient-based refinement around promising points. Bottlenecks of the original coordinate descent are addressed by exploring multiple scales(via clustering) and using adaptive step sizes based on function curvature.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification  \n   We parameterize functions as piecewise constant on a grid (discrete step functions). For a given grid size \\\\(n\\\\):\n   - Let \\\\(\\mathbf{h} = (h_1, h_2, \\ldots, h_n)^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^n\\\\) be the function samples at points \\\\(\\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n\\\\)\n   - Satisfy integral constraint: \\\\(\\sum_{i=1}^n h_i \\cdot \\Delta x = 0.5\\\\) where \\\\(\\Delta x = 2/n\\\\)\n   - Objective: Minimize \\\\(C_5(\\mathbf{h}) = \\max_{k \\in \\{-n+1,\\ldots,n-1\\}} \\sum_{i=1}^{n+k} h_i \\cdot (1 - h_{i+k}) \\cdot \\Delta x\\\\)\n   - Optimization:  \n     \u2022 Coarse Search: Cluster candidate solutions via \\\\(k\\\\)-means to identify representative directions  \n     \u2022 Local Refinement: Use gradient descent on projected function batches with adaptive learning rates  \n     \u2022 Normalization: Ensure feasibility via \\\\(project\\_feasible()\\\\) after each update  \n\n3. Implementation Procedure  \n   a. Initialization:  \n      1. Start with `initial_h_values` of length 700 (if available)  \n      2. Project to satisfy integral constraint via `project_feasible(h, n)`  \n      3. Compute initial C5 via `compute_c5(h, n)`  \n   b. Population-based search:  \n      1. Generate 10-20 diverse solutions using random mutations  \n      2. Cluster candidates using k-means on function batch embeddings  \n      3. Sample top k prototypes per cluster  \n   c. For each prototype:  \n      1. Scale to target integral  \n      2. Perform line search along gradient descent direction  \n      3. Project and refine with coordinate descent  \n   d. Accept improvements via stochastic selection  \n   e. Termination: Stop when time exceeds budget or no improvement  \n\n4. Search Choices  \n   a. k-means: Prototype selection from KNeighbors-based (k=300) embeddings  \n   b. Mutation: Random subsampling + jitter (magnitude=0.03-0.05)  \n   c. Refinement: Adam optimizer with adaptive lr=0.001-0.01 for continuous projected variables  \n   d. Scale factors: 1.0 (initial) and 1.2 (perturbations) for interval [0,2]  \n\n5. Budget and Robustness  \n   - Total budget: 1100 seconds  \n   - Monitoring: Check remaining time every 100 iterations  \n   - Cold start: 200 iterations allocated to clustering  \n   - Load shedding: Reduce population size if time<300 seconds  \n   - Validation: Project candidates during each phase  \n\n6. Validation and Return Contract  \n   - Post-evaluation: Verify feasible via `project_feasible()`  \n   - Compute objective: `compute_c5(h, n)` on original grid  \n   - Selection criteria: Lower C5 values preferred  \n   - Failure handling: Return initial solution if time expires  \n   - Metrics: Joint feasibility + objective value + integral verification",
